{
  "slug": "artist:willie-colon",
  "name": "Willie Colón",
  "editorial_article": "Born in the South Bronx in 1950, Willie Colón became a foundational architect of salsa music after signing with Fania Records at age 15. A trombonist influenced by the hard-edged playing style of Barry Rogers, he rose to prominence as a duo with Héctor Lavoe, eventually recording best-selling collaborations with artists like Rubén Blades and Celia Cruz. His work often explored the diaspora experience, most notably on the 1971 album Asalto Navideño, which utilized traditional rhythms from Puerto Rico, Cuba, and Brazil to challenge the cultural boundaries of his New York base. Throughout his career, he balanced musical innovation—such as adopting a gangster image for his record covers—with a lifelong commitment to civil rights activism and New York City politics.",
